Figure 2. a) 3D structure of \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\); b) View of a single \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pyrazine})\) 2D sheet in \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\), which is connected by \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{dithiolene})\) units.

sheets are connected in the third dimension by redox-active copper bis(dithiolene) units to form a 3D framework (Figure 2a). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction and magnetic susceptibility confirmed both \(\mathrm{Cu}\) atoms as divalent, as expected for their square-planar coordination environment. Adjacent pdt ligands are orthogonal to one another, and the shortest distance between two parallel pdt ligands is \(6.82\,\mathrm{\AA}\). This distance is much longer than the van der Waals distance between pdt ligands, ruling out the possibility of through-space charge transport. Therefore, charge transport is likely through the \([\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pyrazine})]\) 2D sheets via the copper bis(dithiolene) units, with the high-energy unpaired electron of \(\mathrm{d}^{9}\,\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) likely contributing to the relatively high conductivity of this MOF by increasing charge density. We note, however, that the charge-hopping formalism cannot be ruled out, because the redox active \(\mathrm{Cu}\) dithiolene units are within optimal hopping distance of each other.

Although the crystal structure of \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\) indicates its porosity, it collapses upon desolvation. By replacing \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}^{2-}\) with \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}^{2-}\), Kobayashi et al. reported in 2010

an analogous MOF, \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\), which is permanently porous with a BET surface area of \(385\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Figure 2a).[37] The conductivity of this MOF is only \(1\times10^{-8}\;\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) (film, two-probe, room temperature), however, which is more than four orders of magnitude lower than for \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\). A higher activation energy of \(0.49\;\mathrm{eV}\) was also measured. We speculate that the low conductivity in \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\) may be rationalized by a lower charge density as a result of replacement of half of the \(\mathrm{d}^{9}\,\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) sites with \(\mathrm{d}^{8}\,\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\) sites. The measured electrochemical potential of \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-/1-}\) is \(0.257\;\mathrm{V}\) less negative than the value of \([\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-/1-}\); its weaker oxidizing power supports that the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{\mathrm{II}}\) center does not provide high-energy electrons as effective charge carriers. Upon exposing \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\) to \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) vapor at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), its conductivity increases four orders of magnitude to a saturation conductivity of \(1\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\), and the activation energy drops to \(0.18\;\mathrm{eV}\).[37] Weight analysis established that the \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) loading is small, indicating that the charge transport is through the framework rather than through the \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) guest molecules.[38,39] The increase in conductivity was ascribed to the partial oxidation of \([\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-}\) by \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\), which provides loosely bound unpaired electrons as charge carriers. These studies demonstrate the benefit of using redox-active building units, which may allow for improved electrical conductivity via doping.

Another strategy to realize through-bond charge transport is based on extended inorganic 1D chains or 2D sheets formed by the metal ions and the coordinating atoms of the organic ligands. These can be considered as dimensionally reduced versions of their bulk inorganic counterparts, such as metal oxides, metal pnictides, and metal chalcogenides. This strategy requires proper symmetry, spatial, and energy overlap of the orbitals of the metal ions and the coordinating atoms, the energy overlap may be inferred from the relative electronegativities of the atoms involved. For example, because the electronegativity values for transition metals are better matched with that of sulfur than that of oxygen (redox matching),[40] metal–organic chains or sheets connected through metal–sulfur bonds are expected to provide better charge transport pathways than structurally analogous metal–oxygen chains/sheets. Semiconducting coordination polymers based on metal–sulfur chains have been reported,[41,42] and this idea is also applicable to MOFs.

This concept of redox matching has been illustrated in the \(\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC})\) frameworks \(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\), Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, \(\mathrm{DOBDC}^{4-}=2,5\)-dihydroxybenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate). Also known as the MOF-74 or CPO-27 family of MOFs, these are a set of structurally analogous, permanently porous materials that have one-dimensional structural building units (SBUs) with infinite (-M-O-), chains connected through the phenoxide groups of \(\mathrm{DOBDC}\) (Figure 3).[43–49] Surmising that replacement of the phenoxide O atoms in \(\mathrm{DOBDC}\) with S atoms would lead to MOF-74-type MOFs containing (-Mn-S-)\(_\infty\) SBUs instead of (-Mn-O-)\(_\infty\), Sun et al. treated \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) with 2,5-disulfhydrylbenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid \(\mathrm{(H_{4}D S B D C)}\), and isolated \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\).[50] This material indeed has 1D (-Mn-S-)\(_\infty\) chains and remains permanently porous, exhibiting a BET surface area of \(978\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Figure 3). TRMC and TOF measurements showed that the intrinsic charge mobility of
