the activated \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) is \(0.01\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\,\mathrm{V}^{-1}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . This value is comparable to the charge mobility found in organic semiconductors measured by the same techniques.[26]

Although \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) has a relatively high charge mobility, its charge density is expectedly very low because the ligand \(\mathrm{DSBDC}^{4-}\) is not a radical, and the \(\mathrm{d}^{5}\,\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\) ions do not contribute minority-spin electrons.[57] Accordingly, the conductivity of both DMF-coordinated \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) \((2.5\times\) \(10^{-12}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) , pressed pellet, two-probe, \(297\;\mathrm{K}\) ) and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\) - (DOBDC) \((3.9\times10^{-13}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) , pressed-pellet, two-probe, \(297\;\mathrm{K})\) is very low. However, replacing \(\mathrm{d}^{5}\,\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{II}}\) by \(\mathrm{d}^{6}\,\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers introduces high energy, loosely bound minority-spin carriers[51] such that DMF-coordinated \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC})\) (Figure 3b) exhibit conductivity of \(3.9\times\) \(10^{-6}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) (pressed pellet, two-probe, \(297\;\mathrm{K}\) ) and \(3.2\times\) \(10^{-7}\,\mathrm{S\,cm}^{-1}\) (pressed pellet, two-probe, \(297\;\mathrm{K}\) ), respectively.[52] The addition of a single minority-spin carrier thus induces a remarkable increase in conductivity by six orders of magnitude. Although these materials conceptually fall in the "through-bond" formalism, band-structure calculations show that their valence and conduction bands are narrow (dispersion of \(<100\;\mathrm{meV}\) ), indicating that charge transport is

better described by hopping. However, whereas Fe d orbitals dominate the valence band in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC})\) , such that holes likely hop between \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers with little contribution from bridging O atoms, both Fe and S orbitals feature prominently in the valence band of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) , with holes likely using both and likely hopping more easily through the \((-\mathrm{Fe}{-}\mathrm{S})_{\infty}\) chains. Notwithstanding, the minority-spin electron of high spin \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) is a key factor that leads to relatively high electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC})\) , and provides important insight for designing other materials in this class.

The benefit of using \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) is also reflected in \(\mathrm{Fe}(1,2,3-\) triazolate)2, a material reported by G andara et al. in 2012.[53] In this MOF, \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) and 1,2,3-triazolate form a 3D porous network wherein charges likely hop between \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers, as inferred from the band-structure calculation of a 1D Fe(1,2,3- triazolate) coordination polymer (Figure 4).[54] The short Fe–

