computed density of states (DOS) of the TCNQ-infiltrated \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) indicates close proximity between the valenceband maximum of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) and the LUMO of TCNQ, giving rise to a very small activation energy of \(0.041\;\mathrm{eV}.\) As a result, partial charge transfer of \(0.3–0.4\;\mathrm{e}^{-}\) that creates loosely bound charge carriers occurs between the framework and the TCNQ molecules, as confirmed by Raman spectroscopy. The positive sign of the Seebeck coefficient measured for TCNQ-infiltrate \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) \((S=375\,\upmu\mathrm{V}\,\mathrm{K}^{-1}\) at room temperature)[58] confirmed holes as the majority charge carriers. Illustrative of the negative effects of guest incorporation on porosity, TCNQ insertion turns \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) from an insulator into a conductor, but also causes a significant reduction in the BET surface area from \(1844\pm4\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for pristine \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) to \(214\pm0.5\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), consistent with pore occlusion by TCNQ guests.

Common to the examples discussed in this section is that the nature of the metal ions plays a key role in through-bond charge transport. Loosely bound electrons are critical for high conductivity in \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\), \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\)-doped \(\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\)-(DSBDC), and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{DOBDC})\). This is an important and potentially useful concept, but more systematic studies are required to understand the contribution of metal ions to electrical conductivity from both experimental and theoretical perspectives. Future studies may inquire whether \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) is unique in promoting good conductivity, or whether other transition-metal ions with unevenly occupied d levels could fulfill the same role, for instance. More generally, we anticipate a higher prominence of metal ions with loosely bound electrons, such as square-planar \(\mathrm{Cu}^{\mathrm{II}}\) and octahedral

high-spin \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) centers, and of redox-active organic ligands in the design of conductive MOFs. On a practical note, it may be expected that materials containing metal ions, such as high-spin \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\), are less stable under ambient conditions. However, such limitations could be circumvented by various processing techniques (such as the use of impermeable coatings); additionally, the observation of robust atmospheric stability for \(\mathrm{Fe}(1,2,3\)-triazolate), demonstrates that the development of conductive MOFs based on \(\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}\) does not necessarily impose practical limitations.

Charge transfer between guest molecules and metal ions or organic ligands is also a promising method for creating both charge-transport pathways and high charge density, as observed for TCNQ-infiltrated \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\). Perhaps more so than in the case of MOFs with intrinsic conductivity, computational methods will become instrumental for screening host–guest pairs that can give rise to conductivity through guest insertion/intercalation. Indeed, computational studies have recently identified 45 additional MOFs that should become conductive upon TCNQ infiltration.[60] Because the through-bond charge transport strategy is amenable to reticular synthesis, rigorous inspection of existing MOFs may yield additional examples that could become conductive with minimal modification, as was the case for MOF-74.

# 4.2. The "Through-Space" Approach to Charge Transport

Inspired by molecular and polymeric organic conductors and semiconductors, such as TTF–TCNQ (TTF = tetrathiafulvalene)[61] and polythiophene,[62,63] the through-space approach has also been used to design conductive MOFs. This approach aims to use non-covalent interactions, such as \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking between organic ligands, to create an extended charge-transport pathway using a rigid MOF structure that enforces close packing and sufficient orbital overlap between the adjacent ligands.[64] Ideally, this approach can give rise to band-like transport, as in TTF–TCNQ itself and other organic metals. Stable organic radicals are the best candidates for ligands because they provide free charge carriers, thereby improving charge density. A large body of literature exists on the use of this approach to design conductive coordination polymers or molecular conductors,[65,66] many of which display conductivity values in excess of \(1\;\mathrm{S}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).[67–71] To our knowledge, however, none of these exhibit permanent porosity.

The first examples of porous and conductive 3D MOFs designed for through-space transport are a class of TTF-based materials reported by Narayan et al. in 2012, followed by Park et al. in 2015: \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{TTFTB})\) \((\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}\), Co, Zn, and Cd; \(\mathrm{TTFTB}^{+}=\) tetrathiafulvalene tetrabenzoate).[72,73] All four of these MOFs are isostructural, with TTF moieties forming \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacked 1D helical columns with relatively short S···S interactions between neighboring TTF molecules running parallel to infinite metal carboxylate SBUs (Figure 6). They exhibit BET surface areas between 470 and \(540\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{mmol}^{-1}\). DFT calculations show that \(\mathfrak{p}_{z}\) orbitals on the sulfur atoms and the central carbon atoms of the TTF moieties dominate the valence band of \(\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{TTFTB})\). Close contacts between these orbitals on neighboring molecules give rise to some of the
